{"content_hash": "68f0c06fbbc0f987", "version_id": 118, "article_id": 12, "level_code": "ja_n3", "lang": "ja", "level": 3, "level_label": "N3 中級", "title": "地元の作物で、子どもの栄養不足の治療をもっと多くの子に", "title_zh": "用本地种的粮食，让更多营养不良的孩子得到治疗", "topic": "health", "source": "medical_x", "source_url": "https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-locally-grown-crops-access-childhood.html", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://scx1.b-cdn.net/csz/news/tmb/2019/1-soybean.jpg", "credit": ""}, "published_date": "2026-10-06", "word_count": 480, "reading_minutes": 1.6, "lead": "世界には、重度の栄養不足で苦しむ子どもがたくさんいます。でも、効果のある治療食は、そのうちの約30％の子どもにしか届いていません。新しい研究が、地元で育てた作物を使う方法を提案しています。", "lead_zh": "世界上有很多孩子正遭受严重营养不良的折磨。可是，有效的治疗食品只能送到其中约30%的孩子手里。一项新研究提出，可以用当地种植的作物来解决问题。", "preview": "重度の栄養不足の子どもには、特別な治療食「RUTF」が使われます。しかし、今の治療食は、粉ミルクなど、お金のかかる輸入の材料に頼っているので、費用も供給も問題になります。そのため、必要な子どもの約30％にしか届いていません。", "preview_zh": "对于严重营养不良的孩子，会使用一种叫“RUTF”的特殊治疗食品。可是，现在的治疗食品依赖奶粉等昂贵的进口原料，所以费用和供应都成问题。因此，只能送到约30%需要帮助的孩子手中。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 108, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@17fa5ff/content/audio/12/ja_n3.mp3", "duration": 110.587, "size_bytes": 667296, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "ja-JP-NanamiNeural", "word_count": 328, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@17fa5ff/content/data/versions/118.json", "paragraphs": ["重度の栄養不足の子どもには、特別な治療食「RUTF」が使われます。しかし、今の治療食は、粉ミルクなど、お金のかかる輸入の材料に頼っているので、費用も供給も問題になります。そのため、必要な子どもの約30％にしか届いていません。", "ヘブライ大学などの研究チームは、ひよこ豆、ソルゴー、トウモロコシ、大豆で作る治療食「ChSMS」を試しました。これはミルクとピーナッツを使わない植物性の治療食です。動物を使った実験では、売られている治療食より、体重や成長の回復が良くなりました。", "研究によると、ChSMSはたんぱく質が多く、たんぱく質の質の点数も高いです。エネルギーに占めるたんぱく質の割合は約14％で、売られている製品は10％でした。質の点数は87％で、製品は69％でした。", "研究チームは、栄養の質だけでなく「届けやすさ」も大切だと言います。地元で作れる材料を使えば、高い輸入品への依存を減らせるからです。また、国際的なガイドラインも変わり、たんぱく質の半分を乳製品から取る必要がなくなりました。", "この方法は、輸入を減らし、生産費を下げ、地元の経済も助けます。さらに、体重だけでなく身長の伸びにも良い兆しがありました。ただし、これは動物での実験なので、子どもでの効果はこれから臨床研究で確かめる必要があります。"], "paragraphs_zh": ["对于严重营养不良的孩子，会使用一种叫“RUTF”的特殊治疗食品。可是，现在的治疗食品依赖奶粉等昂贵的进口原料，所以费用和供应都成问题。因此，只能送到约30%需要帮助的孩子手中。", "希伯来大学等研究团队试验了用鹰嘴豆、高粱、玉米和大豆制作的“ChSMS”治疗食品。这是不使用牛奶和花生的植物性治疗食品。在动物实验中，它比市售治疗食品更能帮助恢复体重和生长。", "研究显示，ChSMS蛋白质更多，蛋白质质量评分也更高。能量中蛋白质的比例约为14%，而市售产品为10%。质量评分为87%，市售产品为69%。", "研究团队说，除了营养质量，“容易送到”也很重要。因为使用当地能种的材料，就能减少对昂贵进口品的依赖。此外，国际指南也变了，不再要求一半蛋白质必须来自乳制品。", "这种方法能减少进口、降低生产成本，还能帮助当地经济。而且，不只是体重，身高增长也出现了好迹象。不过，这是动物实验，对孩子是否有效，还需要今后的临床研究来确认。"], "body": "重度の栄養不足の子どもには、特別な治療食「RUTF」が使われます。しかし、今の治療食は、粉ミルクなど、お金のかかる輸入の材料に頼っているので、費用も供給も問題になります。そのため、必要な子どもの約30％にしか届いていません。\n\nヘブライ大学などの研究チームは、ひよこ豆、ソルゴー、トウモロコシ、大豆で作る治療食「ChSMS」を試しました。これはミルクとピーナッツを使わない植物性の治療食です。動物を使った実験では、売られている治療食より、体重や成長の回復が良くなりました。\n\n研究によると、ChSMSはたんぱく質が多く、たんぱく質の質の点数も高いです。エネルギーに占めるたんぱく質の割合は約14％で、売られている製品は10％でした。質の点数は87％で、製品は69％でした。\n\n研究チームは、栄養の質だけでなく「届けやすさ」も大切だと言います。地元で作れる材料を使えば、高い輸入品への依存を減らせるからです。また、国際的なガイドラインも変わり、たんぱく質の半分を乳製品から取る必要がなくなりました。\n\nこの方法は、輸入を減らし、生産費を下げ、地元の経済も助けます。さらに、体重だけでなく身長の伸びにも良い兆しがありました。ただし、これは動物での実験なので、子どもでの効果はこれから臨床研究で確かめる必要があります。", "body_zh": "对于严重营养不良的孩子，会使用一种叫“RUTF”的特殊治疗食品。可是，现在的治疗食品依赖奶粉等昂贵的进口原料，所以费用和供应都成问题。因此，只能送到约30%需要帮助的孩子手中。\n\n希伯来大学等研究团队试验了用鹰嘴豆、高粱、玉米和大豆制作的“ChSMS”治疗食品。这是不使用牛奶和花生的植物性治疗食品。在动物实验中，它比市售治疗食品更能帮助恢复体重和生长。\n\n研究显示，ChSMS蛋白质更多，蛋白质质量评分也更高。能量中蛋白质的比例约为14%，而市售产品为10%。质量评分为87%，市售产品为69%。\n\n研究团队说，除了营养质量，“容易送到”也很重要。因为使用当地能种的材料，就能减少对昂贵进口品的依赖。此外，国际指南也变了，不再要求一半蛋白质必须来自乳制品。\n\n这种方法能减少进口、降低生产成本，还能帮助当地经济。而且，不只是体重，身高增长也出现了好迹象。不过，这是动物实验，对孩子是否有效，还需要今后的临床研究来确认。", "text": "重度の栄養不足の子どもには、特別な治療食「RUTF」が使われます。しかし、今の治療食は、粉ミルクなど、お金のかかる輸入の材料に頼っているので、費用も供給も問題になります。そのため、必要な子どもの約30％にしか届いていません。 ヘブライ大学などの研究チームは、ひよこ豆、ソルゴー、トウモロコシ、大豆で作る治療食「ChSMS」を試しました。これはミルクとピーナッツを使わない植物性の治療食です。動物を使った実験では、売られている治療食より、体重や成長の回復が良くなりました。 研究によると、ChSMSはたんぱく質が多く、たんぱく質の質の点数も高いです。エネルギーに占めるたんぱく質の割合は約14％で、売られている製品は10％でした。質の点数は87％で、製品は69％でした。 研究チームは、栄養の質だけでなく「届けやすさ」も大切だと言います。地元で作れる材料を使えば、高い輸入品への依存を減らせるからです。また、国際的なガイドラインも変わり、たんぱく質の半分を乳製品から取る必要がなくなりました。 この方法は、輸入を減らし、生産費を下げ、地元の経済も助けます。さらに、体重だけでなく身長の伸びにも良い兆しがありました。ただし、これは動物での実験なので、子どもでの効果はこれから臨床研究で確かめる必要があります。", "vocab": [{"word": "栄養不足", "pos": "noun", "zh": "营养不良", "note": "常和「重度の」搭配，表示严重程度"}, {"word": "治療食", "pos": "noun", "zh": "治疗食品", "note": "本文中指给营养不良孩子吃的特殊食品"}, {"word": "頼る", "pos": "verb", "zh": "依赖、依靠", "note": "常以「〜に頼る」的形式使用"}, {"word": "輸入", "pos": "noun", "zh": "进口", "note": "反义词是「輸出（出口）」"}, {"word": "材料", "pos": "noun", "zh": "原料、材料", "note": "做东西时用的东西"}, {"word": "植物性", "pos": "noun", "zh": "植物性", "note": "和「動物性」相对"}, {"word": "実験", "pos": "noun", "zh": "实验", "note": "本文指动物实验"}, {"word": "回復", "pos": "noun", "zh": "恢复", "note": "常和「する」一起用"}, {"word": "たんぱく質", "pos": "noun", "zh": "蛋白质", "note": "身体需要的营养成分"}, {"word": "依存", "pos": "noun", "zh": "依赖", "note": "常和「する」一起用，如「輸入に依存する」"}, {"word": "臨床研究", "pos": "noun", "zh": "临床研究", "note": "在人的身上进行的研究"}, {"word": "確認", "pos": "noun", "zh": "确认", "note": "常和「する」一起用"}], "title_original": "Locally grown crops could expand access to childhood malnutrition treatment", "summary_original": "For millions of children suffering from severe malnutrition, an effective treatment already exists. The problem is that many of them never receive it.", "body_original": "Locally grown crops could expand access to childhood malnutrition treatment\nby\nHebrew University of Jerusalem\nedited by\nRobert Egan\nEditors' notes\nThis article has been reviewed according to Science X's\neditorial process\nand\npolicies\n.\nEditors\nhave highlighted\nthe following attributes while ensuring the content's credibility:\nfact-checked\ntrusted source\nproofread\nThe GIST\nAdd as preferred source\nFor millions of children suffering from severe malnutrition, an effective treatment already exists. The problem is that many of them never receive it.\nA new study led by Dr. Aurélie Bechoff, a visiting researcher at the Hebrew University of Jerusalem, and Hebrew University professors Efrat Monsonego-Ornan and Ram Reifen, together with an international research team, suggests that part of the solution may lie in producing therapeutic food closer to where it is needed, using crops that are already grown locally. The study,\npublished\nin\nCurrent Developments in Nutrition\n, examined a high-protein therapeutic food made from chickpeas, sorghum, corn and soybeans, ingredients widely available in sub-Saharan Africa.\nReady-to-use therapeutic foods, known as RUTFs, are a standard treatment for children with severe acute malnutrition. Yet according to the study, current treatment reaches only around 30% of children who need it. One of the major challenges is that conventional formulations often depend on ingredients produced in wealthier countries, including relatively expensive powdered milk, creating both cost and supply barriers in lower-income countries.\nThose barriers are becoming increasingly urgent as conflict, climate change and cuts to global humanitarian funding place additional pressure on food and nutrition programs. The researchers argue that developing effective therapeutic foods from locally available ingredients could help make treatment less dependent on international supply chains and more affordable to produce in the regions where it is most needed.\nThe formulation tested in the study, known as ChSMS, replaces milk and peanuts with a combination of chickpeas, sorghum, corn and soybeans. In the preclinical study, it supported substantially better recovery from malnutrition than the commercial peanut-and-milk formulation tested, including catch-up growth.\nThe researchers found that the plant-based formulation contained more protein and had a higher protein quality score than the commercial product. ChSMS provided around 14% of its energy from protein, compared with 10% in the commercial formulation, while its protein quality score reached 87%, compared with 69%.\nBut for the researchers, the significance of the formulation goes beyond its nutritional composition.\n\"Access is one of the biggest challenges in treating childhood malnutrition,\" said Monsonego-Ornan of the Hebrew University of Jerusalem. \"If therapeutic foods can be produced from high-quality ingredients that are already grown locally, we may be able to reduce dependence on costly imports and make treatment available to many more children. The nutritional quality has to remain the priority, but affordability and local production are essential if we want effective solutions to reach the populations that need them most.\"\nInternational guidelines have also begun to make such approaches more feasible. Earlier requirements called for at least half of the protein in therapeutic foods to come from dairy products, but newer guidelines have removed that requirement, opening the door to locally sourced, nondairy formulations that meet nutritional standards.\nThat shift could have broader economic consequences as well. Because the new formulation is dairy-free, the researchers say it could eliminate the need for expensive imported powdered milk in its production. Using crops such as soybeans, chickpeas, sorghum and corn could instead support local food manufacturers, agricultural supply chains and smallholder farmers in sub-Saharan Africa.\nThe approach could therefore address several challenges at once: lowering dependence on imported ingredients, reducing production costs, strengthening local economies and potentially making lifesaving nutritional treatment available to more children.\nThe researchers also see potential for locally produced therapeutic foods to play a broader role in addressing different forms of malnutrition. The study found signs of recovery in linear growth as well as weight, raising the possibility that future formulations could address both acute malnutrition and longer-term stunting. A product capable of targeting both conditions could provide a more efficient and cost-effective approach to a major global health challenge.\nThe findings are preclinical and will need to be confirmed in clinical studies before conclusions can be drawn about the formulation's effectiveness in children. However, the researchers say the results provide an important foundation for developing therapeutic foods that combine strong nutritional quality with affordability, local availability and long-term sustainability.\nThe study involved researchers from the Hebrew University of Jerusalem, the University of Greenwich, the University of Kent, Benue State University, the University of Nottingham and partner organizations in Kenya and Ireland.\nMore information\nAurélie Bechoff et al, Local High-Protein, Plant-Based Ready-to-Use Therapeutic Food Enhances Recovery from Malnutrition in Rats,\nCurrent Developments in Nutrition\n(2026).\nDOI: 10.1016/j.cdnut.2026.109437\nClinical categories\nPediatrics\nChildren's health\nNutrition & Healthy eating\nCommon illnesses & Prevention\nProvided by\nHebrew University of Jerusalem\nWho's behind this story?\nRobert Egan\nBachelor's in mathematical biology, Master's in creative writing. Well-traveled with unique perspectives on science and language.\nFull profile →\nCitation\n:\nLocally grown crops could expand access to childhood malnutrition treatment (2026, October 6)\nretrieved 6 October 2026\nfrom https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-locally-grown-crops-access-childhood.html\nThis document is subject to copyright. Apart from any fair dealing for the purpose of private study or research, no\npart may be reproduced without the written permission. The content is provided for information purposes only.", "fetched_at": "2026-10-07T00:52:23.375860", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "重度", "s": 0.125, "e": 0.512, "sm": 125, "em": 512, "cs": 0, "ce": 2, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "の", "s": 0.525, "e": 0.625, "sm": 525, "em": 625, "cs": 2, "ce": 3, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "栄養", "s": 0.637, "e": 1.112, "sm": 637, "em": 1112, "cs": 3, "ce": 5, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "不足", "s": 1.125, "e": 1.488, "sm": 1125, "em": 1487, "cs": 5, "ce": 7, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "の", "s": 1.5, "e": 1.613, "sm": 1500, "em": 1612, "cs": 7, "ce": 8, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "子ども", "s": 1.625, "e": 1.962, "sm": 1625, 